      {
        "id": 87390,
        "name": "Greywind",
        "username": "Greywind",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/greywind/{size}/2006_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-23T19:54:39.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Envisioner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Envisioner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I've never understood the numbers.&nbsp; 1, 3, 7 and 9?&nbsp; Why not 5?&nbsp; Why no even numbers?&nbsp; Why did the elves get fewer rings even though they live longer?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Sauron forged the One in secret and imbued it with so much of his power that he could not survive without it. Hence his dissolution when it was destroyed.</p>\n<p>Celebrimbor forged the Three without the assistance of Annatar (Sauron). Once Sauron forged the One, the elves wearing the Three knew they had been betrayed and hid the Three away. The elves, being immortal and having suffered through Morgoth, did not give in to the darkness. There are three branches of elves.</p>\n<p>The Seven were given to the leaders of the seven dwarf clans. Mostly they ended up being dragon magnets.</p>\n<p>The Nine were given to powerful kings of men. The rings did not give more life. They simply stretched what was already there to accommodate continued existence.</p>",
