      {
        "id": 87393,
        "name": "Nielzabub",
        "username": "Nielzabub",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/nielzabub/{size}/1986_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-23T23:48:47.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>@Ameena</p><p>Yes, I know the rings are important to character motivation and the like, but most of the time it doesn't seem to hurt an understanding of the story if you don't know about the specific rings and their effects. Dwarves had treasure, Smaug stole it, they want it back. That motivation can be understood without the background information of the ring. It's cool to know, and it definitely adds to the story, but knowing that piece of information doesn't seem to be critical to understanding the plot. This is true of most stuff in Tokien though, like Shadowfax's geneaology.</p><p>This can be shown by the LOTR movies that more or less removed most of the mention of the other rings, except in the extended edition, and were still widely popular with the audience.</p>",
