      {
        "id": 87396,
        "name": "phantaskippy",
        "username": "phantaskippy",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/{size}/1897_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-24T02:51:29.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Tolkien was a Linguist who loved Mythology.</p><p>The stories and world are based on the languages he created for them.</p><p>He wasn't really a writer*, in the sense that he created leanguages, then a world that they came from and the Mythology that led those groups to be what they were and the stories flow from that. &nbsp;Most writers create a world to support a story, Tolkien created a world to hold the languages he wanted to write. &nbsp;He also wrote the Lord of the Rings because he felt England didn't have a good enough Mythology.</p><p>His abandoned Arthurian Poem was as much an exploration of old English poetry as anything else, he seems to have had a love/hate relationship with Arthurian Legend, as the major Mythology of the English it was important to him, but he seems disappointed by it, and especially the French influences. &nbsp;(I agree there, the Grail and Lancelot suck)</p><p>At least that is what I get from him.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>*What I mean here is that his stories were always about the languages and exploring the people that made them, Mythology as important shared history has great impact on language, so he created languages, then created the mythology of the people that belong to that language.</p>",
        "post_number": 28,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:11:40.260Z",
