      {
        "id": 87397,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-24T06:15:05.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"McBehrer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mcbehrer/48/1850_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> McBehrer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Well JRR Tolkien is Catholic. So yeah, Buddhism has the Eightfold path, but that's not relevant to HIM, so why would you expect him to care enough to include it?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Never said I did.&nbsp; Like I said, we have \"catholic\" as the answer as to why he didn't use other numbers, but without that answer, there was no reason for him to think some numbers were more special than others.</p>",
